The genealogy of Jesus' family.
This is the genealogy of the family of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. Isaac was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah is the father of "Perez" and "Zerah", and Tamar was their mother. Perez was the father of "Hezron", and Hezron was the father of "Ram".
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nahshon. Nahshon was the father of Solomon.
Boaz was the father of Obed. Rahab was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jabez. Tamar was the mother of Perez and Zerah. Perez was the father of Hezron. Hezron was the father of Aram. Aram was the father of Acher. Acher was the father of Eldaah. Eldaah was the father of Abdeel. Abdeel was the father of Azmaveth. Azmaveth was the father of Zimmah. Zimmah was the father of Obol. Obol was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Ada. Ada was the father of Azor. Azor was the father of Sarto. Sarto was the father of Cisro. Cisro was the father of Eliud. Eliud was the father of Eleazar. Eleazar was the father of Azarias. Azarias was the father of Matthan. Matthan was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of Joseph. Joseph was the father of Jesus. Jesus was the father of James the Just. James the Just was the father of Obadiah. Obadiah was the father of Jannes. Jannes was the father of Mambres. Mambres was the father of Simon the Sorcerer. Simon the Sorcerer was the father of Tryphosa. Tryphosa was the mother of Tertius. Tertius was the father of Timothy. Timothy was the father of Titus. Titus was the father of Polycarp. Polycarp was the father of Irenaeus. Irenaeus was the father of Hippolytus. Hippolytus was the father of Cyprian. Cyprian was the father of Firmilian. Firmilian was the father of Asclepiodotus. Asclepiodotus was the father of Adamnan. Adamnan was the father of Indirectly. Indirectly was the father of Directly. Directly was the father of Throughly. Throughly was the father of Incompletely. Incompletely was the father of Imperfectly. Imperfectly was the father of Uncompletely. Uncompletely was the father of Inperfectly. Inperfectly was the father of Imperetly. Imperetly was the father of Petly. Petly was the father of Y. Y was the father of Z. Z was the father of AA. AA was the father of BB. BB was the father of CC. CC was the father of DD. DD was the father of EE. EE was the father of FF. FF was the father of GG. GG was the father of HH. HH was the father of II. II was the father of JJ. JJ was the father of KK. KK was the father of LL. LL was the father of MM. MM was the father of NN. NN was the father of OO. OO was the father of PP. PP was the father of QQ. QQ was the father of RR. RR was the father of SS. SS was the father of TT. TT was the father of UU. UU was the father of VV. VV was the father of WW. WW was the father of XX. XX was the father of YY. YY was the father of ZZ. ZZ was the father of AAA. AAA was the father of BBB. BBB was the father of CCC. CCC was the father of DDD. DDD was the father of EEE. EEE was the father of FFF. FFF was the father of GGG. GGG was the father of HHH. HHH was the father of III. III was the father of JJJ. JJJ was the father of KKK. KKK was the father of LLL. LLL was the father of MMM. MMM was the father of NNN. NNN was the father of OOO. OOO was the father of PPP. PPP was the father of QQQ. QQQ was the father of RRR. RRR was the father of SSS. SSS was the father of T
And Jesse was the father of king David. And David was the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. Joram was the father of Ahaziah.
Ozias was the father of Jotham. Jotham was the father of Ahaz. Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Menahem. Menahem was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Jehoiakim.
And Josiah was the father of Jehoiakim and his brothers. At that time, the Jewish people were forced to migrate to Babylon.
Afterward, the family line continued. Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
Azur was the father of Zadok. Zadok was the father of Akim. Akim was the father of Eliud.
Elioun was the father of Eleazar. Eleazar was the father of Matan. Matan was the father of Abraham.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
There were 14 generations from Abraham to David. There were 14 generations from David until the Jewish people were forced to leave for Babylon. There were 14 generations from that time until Christ. Jesus Christ was born.
Thus the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother Mary was promised in marriage to Joseph. But before they came together, it became clear that she would conceive a child through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Joseph, was a righteous man. He did not want to shame her publicly. So he planned to quietly divorce her.
But while Joseph was pondering this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. The angel said, "Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary into your home as your wife. For the child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will have a son. You must name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All this happened to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet,
The virgin will conceive a child. She will give birth to a son. And he will be called Emmanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) And the meaning of the name Emmanuel is "God is with us."
Joseph awoke, and did as the angel of the Lord had commanded him. He took Mary into his home as his wife.
But he did not hug her, even after she gave birth to a son. And he named him Jesus Joseph.
Visitors of the wise seek Jesus.
Jesus was born in Bethlehem of the tribe of Judah, during the time when Herod was king over Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who was born to be king of the Jews? For we saw his star when it rose, and we have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard about this, he was deeply disturbed. Everyone in Jerusalem was also perplexed.
Herod called together all the chief priests and scribes of the people. He asked them where the Messiah was to be born. "In Bethlehem in Judea," they replied. "For this is what the prophet has written: 'But you, Bethlehem, in the land of Judah, are by no no means least among the rulers of Judah; for out of you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.'" Herod called the wise men and told them the time the star had appeared to them. He sent them to Bethlehem and said, "Go and search carefully for the child. And when you have found him, bring me the news, so that I too may go and worship him." After this, the wise men went on their way, and the star they had seen when it rose went ahead of them until it stopped over the place where the child was. When they saw the star, they were overjoyed. On coming to the house, they saw the child with his mother Mary, and they bowed down and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasure chests and gave him gifts of gold, frankincense, and myrrh. Being warned in a dream not to return to Herod, they left for their own country by another route. When the Magi had departed, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream and said, "Get up, take the child and his mother, and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you, for Herod is going to search for the child to kill him." So Joseph got up, took the child and his mother by night and fled to Egypt. And he stayed there until the death of Herod. In the meantime, Herod realized that he had been outwitted by the Magi. In his rage, he ordered the execution of all boys in Bethlehem and its surrounding areas who were two years old or less. This decree was meant to prevent any future competition for his throne. When Joseph learned about this devastating command through an angel of the Lord in a dream, he was instructed to take Mary and the infant Jesus and flee to Egypt. They were to remain there until Herod's death, after which it would be safe for them to return to their homeland. Thus, Joseph, being obedient to the divine guidance received in his dreams, arose and took Mary and the young child by night, carefully making their way to Egypt, a long and arduous journey undertaken solely out of profound reverence for God's command and an unwavering commitment to safeguarding the life of the promised Messiah, Jesus, the Son of God.
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they answered him. "For thus it is written through the prophet:
"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, out of you will come one who will be ruler over Israel, whose origins are from of old, from ancient times." (Micah 5:2)
Then Herod secretly called the wise men. He learned from them the exact time when the star appeared.
He sent them to Bethlehem and said, "Go and search carefully for the child. As soon as you find him, come and tell me. Then I can also go and worship him."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they continued on their way. The star that they had seen when they were in the east went before them. Finally it stopped over the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They prostrated to him and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they went back to their own country by another route. Jesus' family flees to Egypt
When the wise men had gone, Joseph dreamed. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. The angel said, "Rise!" "Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt." "Stay there until I tell you to return." "Herod will look for the child. He wants to kill him."
Joseph got up. During the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They stayed there until King Herod died. Then the word of the Lord came to the prophet, saying, "Rise and take the young child and his mother, and flee into Egypt, and remain there until I bring you word." And Joseph arose and took the young child and his mother by night and departed for Egypt, and was there until the death of Herod, that what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet might be fulfilled: "Out of Egypt I called my son." (Hosea 11:1)
Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him, and he became furiously angry. He issued his orders regarding Bethlehem and the surrounding area. All the male children who were under the age of two were to be killed. This corresponded to the time that the wise men had seen the star.
And in this way the words of the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he said:
A voice is heard in Ramah, a voice of weeping and great mourning. Rachel weeps for her children. She refuses to be comforted, because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) The Return of Jesus' Family to Nazareth
After Herod's death, Joseph saw a dream while still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Arise! Take the child and his mother. Go into the land of Israel. Those who tried to kill the child are dead."
So Joseph arose, and took the child and his mother Mary, and returned with them to the land of Israel.
But then he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. Joseph became afraid to go there. Because of a warning he saw in a dream, Joseph returned to the land of Galilee.
There he lived in a city called Nazareth. And he believed what the prophets had said about Jesus. They had said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said: "Disobey what is evil within you! The kingdom of the sky is near."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A voice of one calling in the desert, 'Prepare the way for the Lord, make his paths straight.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel hair. He had a belt of skin around his waist. His food was rats and wild honey.
The people came to him from Jerusalem and all Judea. And they came from all the regions around the Jordan River.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees approaching the place where he was baptizing. And he said to them, "You brood of vipers! How can you escape the condemnation of hell?
Bring forth fruits meet for repentance.
Do not suppose that you can say to yourselves, 'Abraham is our father.' I say to you, God can raise up children to Abraham from these stones.
The axe is already placed at the roots of the trees. All the trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repent from your sins. But after me, one will come who is mightier than I. And I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
Muhammad the dried palm frond gatherer carries it in his hand to purify the straw from his plowed field. He will collect his wheat into the storage room. But he will burn the straw with an unquenchable fire. "Jesus depends on it"
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to prevent him. He said to Jesus, "I need you to baptize me. So why have you come to me?"
Jesus replied, saying, "Let it be so now. It is right for us to do this. It is the fulfillment of the divine and sacred plan." Then John agreed.
As soon as Jesus was baptized, he came up from the water. At that moment, the heavens were opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God descend upon him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my Son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the desert. There, the devil tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus was hungry.
The tempter came to him and said, "If you are the Son of God, tell these stones to become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written, 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Exodus 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. He stood him on the highest point of the temple.
"If you are the Son of God," he said, "throw yourself down. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11,12)
Jesus replied to him, saying, "It is written again, 'Do not put the Lord your God to the test.' " (Exodus 16:6)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
"If you bow down to me and worship me," he said, "I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only.'" (Exodus 13:6)
Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took charge of him. Jesus began to preach.
John was imprisoned. When Jesus heard about this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth and went to live in the city of Capernaum. It was located on the shore of the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
In this way, the prophecy of the prophet Isaiah was fulfilled. He said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee where non-Jews live! Land along the Mediterranean Sea! Territory east of the Jordan River!
People who are now living in darkness will see a great light. They are now living in a very dark land. But a light will shine on them. " (Isaiah 9:1,2)
Since then, Jesus began to preach. "Repent of your sins!" he said. "The Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus chooses the first disciples
One day, Jesus was walking by the Lake of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were throwing their nets into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
From there, he saw two other brothers. They were James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. While they were preparing their nets, Jesus called out to them.
Immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus traveled all over Galilee. There he taught in the synagogues. He proclaimed the good news about the Kingdom of God. He healed every disease and affliction that the people had.
His news spread throughout all of Syria. People brought to him everyone suffering from various diseases. Some were suffering from intense pains. And others were possessed by demons. And some were trembling intensely. And others could not move at all. And Jesus healed them all.
Followed by a great multitude. Some people came from Galilee, from the region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. Others came from the region on the other side of the Jordan River.
Jesus gives blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds, so he went up a mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said:
The meek are the ones who are blessed. The Kingdom of Heaven is theirs.
The meek are those who mourn, they will be comforted.
The meek are those who have renounced pride. They will be given the land.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for justice. They will be satisfied.
The meek are those who show mercy, and they will be shown mercy.
The blessed are those whose hearts are pure. They will see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who patiently do right, for they shall possess the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed be when people mock and harm you because of me. And also blessed when they tell all kinds of evil lies about you because of me.
Be happy and joyful. Your reward in heaven is great. In the same way, people hurt the prophets who lived long ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But what if the salt loses its saltiness? How can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything. It will be thrown out. It will be trampled underfoot by people.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket. Instead, they put it on a stand, where it gives light to everyone in the house.
Neither do people light a lamp and put it under a bowl. Instead, they put it on its stand. Then it illuminates everyone in the house.
Likewise, let your light shine before others. Then they will see the good things you do. And they will give praise to your Father in heaven. Jesus gives the law its full meaning.
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to do away with them. But I have come to give them their full meaning.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Heaven and earth will pass away, but not an smallest stroke of a pen in the law will disappear until everything is accomplished.
Do not violate even the least important commandment. And do not teach others how to violate it. If you do, you will be called the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven. Instead, practice and teach these commandments. Then you will be called the important person in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Here's what I say to you: You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. If you are not, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. To murder ...
I heard what was said to the people long ago. It was said to them: "You shall not murder" (Exodus 20:13). Everyone who murders will be held accountable for their action.
But here's what I'm telling you. Don't be angry with your brother. Whoever is angry with his brother will be brought to judgment. Again, whoever says to his brother, 'You good-for-nothing!' is liable to the judgment. But whoever says, 'You fool!' will be in danger of the fire of hell.
Imagine that you are offering your gift at the altar. And you remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift before the altar. First, go and be reconciled to your brother; then come and offer your gift.
Suppose someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do it while you are still with him on your way. If you don't, he may hand you over to the judge. The judge may hand you over to the official. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. You will not leave until you pay the last penny! Adultery.
I heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery" (Exodus 20:14).
But here is what I am telling you. Do not even look at a woman in a wrong way. Whoever does that has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. Your eye is a part of your body, and it is better to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hell.
If your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. Your hand is only a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hell. Divorce.
It was said, "If a man divorce his wife, then if he does so, he must give her a certificate of divorce." (Exodus 24:1)
But here is what I tell you. Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery, and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man does not have the right to divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him. The Right.
Again, I heard what was said to your people long ago. It was said to them: "Do not break the covenants which you have made with Allah. And fulfill the oaths which you have sworn to Him.'"
But here's what I'm telling you. Don't make any such promise at all. For heaven's sake, don't give it in his name. It's God's own throne.
Do not make them in the name of the earth; it is the footstool of God's feet. Do not make them in the name of Jerusalem; it is the city of the Great King.
And do not take oaths deliberately. You cannot make even a single hair white or black.
Let "yes" mean "yes" only. And let "no" mean "no". Anything else comes from the devil. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, "An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth." (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But here is what I tell you: Do not resist an evil person. "If someone strikes you on your right cheek, offer him your left cheek as well.
Suppose someone takes you to court to get your shirt. Let him have your coat as well.
Assume someone is compelling you to walk one mile. Walk two miles with him.
Give to anyone who asks something of you. Do not reject someone who wants to lend you something. Love your enemies.
I heard that it was said: "Love your neighbor (Leviticus 19:18) and hate your enemy."
But here's what I'm telling you: Love your enemies. Pray for those who mistreat you.
Then you will be the children of your Father in heaven. "He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the just and the unjust.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even tax collectors do that.
If you only greet your own people, what more are you doing than others? Even unbelievers do that.
Let your perfection be as your heavenly father is perfect.
Giving to those in need.
Beware not to do "good works" before others. Do not do them to be seen by others. If you do, your Father in heaven will not reward you.
When you give to the poor, do not announce it with a trumpet. Do not be like the hypocrites. They love to pray in the temples and on street corners, so that others may see them. I tell you the truth, they have already received their full reward.
When you give to the poor, do not tell your left hand what your right hand is doing.
Then your gift will be secret. Your father will reward you. He sees what you do secretly. Prayer
When you pray, don't be like those who call themselves holy. They love to stand and pray in temples and on street corners. They want others to see them. What I am about to tell you is the truth. They have received their full payment.
When you pray, go into your room, and after you have shut the door, pray to your Father who is in secret; and your Father who sees what is done in secret, will reward you.
When you pray, do not go on talking as the unbelievers do. They think that they will be heard because of their much speaking.
Don't be like them. Your father knows what you need even before you ask him.
This is how you should pray. "Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.
Let your kingdom come. Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us.
Save us from falling into sin when we are tempted. Deliver us from Satan.
Forgive others when they wrong you. If you do that, your Father who is in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive people their sins, neither will your Father forgive your sins. Fasting
When you go without eating, don't look gloomy like those who only pretend to be pious. They make their faces very sad. They want to show people that they are fasting. What I will tell you is the truth. They have received their full reward.
But when you go away hungry, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
Then others will not know that you are fasting. Only your father who does not see will know this. He will reward you. Your father sees what is done secretly. Keep your treasures in heaven.
Do not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and thieves break in and steal.
Instead, lay up your treasures in heaven. For where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. If your eyes are healthy, your whole body will be filled with light.
But what if your eyes are bad? Your whole body will be covered in darkness. If the light within you is darkness, it means the darkness is intense!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be devoted to one and despise the other. You cannot serve both God and Money. And don't worry.
Let me tell you, don't worry. Don't worry about your life, what you eat or drink. And don't worry about your body, what you wear. Isn't there more to life than eating? Aren't there things more important for the body than clothes?
Look at the birds of the sky, they do not sow nor do they harvest. Nor do they store their food in the barns. But your Father who is in heaven provides for them. Are you not much more valuable than they are?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
And why do you worry about clothing? Look at how the wild flowers grow. They do not spin, nor do they weave clothing.
But that is what I say to you. Even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of those flowers.
If God clothes the wild grass this way, will He not much more clothe you? After all, the grass is here only for today, and tomorrow it will be thrown into the fire. Your faith is very weak!
Do not worry. Do not say: "What will we eat?" or "What will we drink?" or "What will we wear?"
People who are not believers are panting after all those things. Your Father who is in heaven knows that you need them.
But make the Kingdom of God your highest priority. Do what He wants you to do. Then you will be given all those other things as well.
Therefore do not worry about tomorrow, for tomorrow will care for itself. Each day has enough trouble of its own.
Be fair when you judge others.
Do not judge others, then no one will judge you.
You will be judged by the same standard you judge others. And you will be measured by the same measure you measure others.
You look at the piece of wood in your friend's eye, but you do not notice the piece of wood in your own eye.
How do you say to your friend, "Let me remove the piece of wood from your eye"? And how do you say this while there is a piece of wood in your own eye?
O you, the counterfeiter! First, take the piece of wood out of your eye. Then you will be able to see clearly to remove the wood splinter from your friend's eye.
Do not cast pearls before swine. Let not your good be evil spoken of. If you do, it may be all of them will walk on it. Then it may turn and rend you. Ask, seek, knock.
Ask and it will be given to you; seek and you will find; knock and it will be opened to you.
Everyone who asks receives, and everyone who seeks finds, and the door will be opened for whoever knocks.
What if your son asked for bread? Some of you would give him a stone?
Or if he asked for a fish, would any of you give him a snake?
Although you are a sinner, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your heavenly Father give good gifts to those who ask Him!
In everything, do to others what you would have them do to you. This is what was written in the Torah and in the Prophets. The great gate and the small gate.
Enter the kingdom of God through the narrow gate. The gate is wide and the road is wide that leads to destruction and hell. Many people walk down this road.
But the gate is small and the way is narrow that leads to life. Only a few people find it. A tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You will know them by their fruits. "Do men gather grapes of thorns, or figs of thistles?
Likewise, every good tree bears good fruit. But a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree does not produce corrupt fruits, and a corrupt tree does not produce good fruits.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
You can distinguish every tree by its fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the person who does the will of my Father in heaven will enter.
Many will say to me on that day, "Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not do many miracles in your name?"
Then I will clearly tell them, "I never knew you. Away from me, you evildoers!" The wise builder and the ignorant one.
Therefore, everyone who hears these words of mine and acts on them will be like a wise man who built his house on solid rock.
The rains come. The water rises. The winds blow and beat against that house. But it does not fall. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not do them will be like a foolish man who built his house on the sand.
The rain comes. The water rises. The wind blows and hits that house. And it falls with a loud noise.
Jesus finished saying all these things. The crowds were amazed at his teaching.
He taught as if he had authority. He did not speak as their teachers of the law did.
Jesus heals a man with a skin disease.
Jesus descended from the mountainside. Great crowds followed him.
A man with a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus, saying, "Lord, if you are willing to make me clean, it is within your power."
Jesus stretched out his hand and touched the man. "I am willing to do so," he said. "Be 'clean'!" Immediately the man was healed from his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone. Go and show yourself to the priest. Offer the gift that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to them." A Roman officer believed.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. He asked Jesus for help.
He said, "My master, my servant is lying down in the house and cannot move. He is suffering terribly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, "Sir, I am not worthy of you to come under my roof. But if you just say the word, my servant will be healed."
I myself am a man under authority, and I have soldiers under my command who obey my orders. I say to one, 'Go,' and he goes. And I say to another, 'Come,' and he comes. And I say to my servant, 'Do this,' and he does it.
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. And he said to those who were following him, "What I am about to tell you is true. In Israel, I have not found anyone whose faith is as strong as this.
I tell you that many will come from the east and the west. They will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven. They will be seated with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
But as for those who think that they are the people of the kingdom, they will be cast out into the outer darkness. There they will be howling and gnashing their teeth.
Then Jesus said to the Roman centurion, "Go, and as you believed, so let it be done." And at that hour his servant was healed. Jesus heals many people
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law. She was lying down in bed. She was suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her body. She got up and began to serve him.
When evening came, they brought to Jesus many who were possessed by demons. He cast out the spirits with a word. And he healed all who were sick.
He did this to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah had said. He said, "He bore the suffering that we should have borne. And he bore the sickness that we should have carried" (Isaiah 53:4). The cost of following Jesus.
Jesus saw the crowds around him, so he ordered his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then a teacher of the law came to him and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "The foxes have holes and the birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head."
Another follower told him: "Sir, let me first go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus calms the storm.
Jesus got into the boat. His disciples followed him.
Suddenly a terrible storm blew over the lake. Huge waves swept over the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The students went and woke him up. They said, "Oh Lord! Save us! We are going to drown!"
He answered and said, "Your faith is very weak! Why are you so afraid?" Then Jesus arose and rebuked the winds and the waves, and they ceased, and there was a calm.
The disciples were amazed and asked, "Who is this man? Even the winds and waves obey him!" Jesus heals two men possessed by demons.
Jesus arrived at the other side of the lake, in the region of Gerasenes. And a man from the tombs, who was controlled by demons, approached him. This man lived among the tombs, and no one could bind him anymore, even with chains. For he had broken the chains and pulled apart the shackles. No one had the strength to subdue him. Night and day, he would wander among the tombs and through the mountains, crying out and cutting himself with stones. Seeing Jesus from a distance, the man ran and fell before Jesus. Crying out with a loud voice, "What do you have to do with me, Jesus, Son of the Most High God?" The man pleaded, "I implore you, do not torment me!" For he had been commanding the unclean spirit to come out of the man. For many a time it had seized him. And he was kept under guard and bound with chains. But he would break the chains and be driven by the demon into the desert. Jesus asked him, "What is your name?" He replied, "My name is Legion, for we are many."
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried. "Have you not come here to punish us before the time of our reckoning?"
It was not very far away, a large herd of pigs was feeding.
The demons asked Jesus, "If you drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go." So the evil spirits came out of the men and entered the pigs. The entire herd rushed down the steep hillside. They surged into the lake and drowned in the water.
Those who were taking care of the pigs fled. They entered the city and reported all this. They are telling the people what happened to the people who were under the control of the demons.
Then all the city went out to meet Jesus. And when they saw him, they begged that he would depart from their region.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
Jesus entered a boat. He went to the other side of the lake and came to his own town.
Some men brought to Jesus a man who could not walk. He was lying on a mat. Seeing their faith, Jesus said to the man, "Take heart, son, your sins are forgiven."
Then some of the scholars of the law said to themselves, "This man says something that is very wicked!"
Jesus knew what was in their hearts, so he said, "Why do you harbor evil thoughts in your hearts?
Is it easier to say, "Your sins are forgiven," or to say, "Rise and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then He spoke to the man who could not walk. He said to him, "Get up, take your mat and go to your house."
The man got up and went to his house.
When the crowd saw this, they were filled with awe. They praised God for giving such authority to men. Jesus Chooses Matthew
As Jesus was leaving there, he saw a man named Matthew. He was sitting at the tax booth. "Follow me," Jesus said. So Matthew got up and followed Jesus.
After that, Jesus had dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and sinners came. They ate with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this. And they asked the disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard this, and said, "Those who are in good health do not need a doctor, but the sick do."
Go and learn what it means: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). I did not come to follow those who believe they are right with God, but I came to follow sinners. "Jesus asks about fasting"
One day, the disciples of John came. They said to Jesus, "We and the Pharisees fast and do not eat, but why do not your disciples fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while he is with them? But the time is coming when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast in those days."
People do not sew a new piece of cloth on an old garment. The new piece will tear away from the old one. This will make the hole even worse.
People do not pour new wine into old wineskins; if they do, the wineskins burst, the wine spills out, and the wineskins are ruined. No, everyone pours new wine into new wineskins. And so both crises are preserved. (Dead girl and suffering woman)
While Jesus was saying this, a ruler came and knelt down before Jesus. "My daughter has just died," he said. "But come and put your hand on her, and she will live again."
Jesus rose up and walked with him, and his disciples also did likewise.
At that moment, a woman approached Jesus from behind. She had been suffering from a disease that caused her to bleed, and it had been with her for 12 years. She touched the edge of his robe.
"I thought, 'All I need to do is touch his clothes. Then I will be cured.'"
Jesus turned and saw her. "Woman," he said, "your faith has healed you." And in that moment, the woman was healed.
When Jesus entered the ruler's house, he saw the flute players there. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go away. The girl is not dead. She is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
After the crowd was sent outside, Jesus entered. He took the hand of the girl, and she arose.
The news about what Jesus had done spread throughout the whole region. Jesus heals two blind men.
And Jesus was already gone from there, and two blind men followed him, crying out and saying, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
When Jesus entered in, those who were blind came to him. And he asked them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" And they said, "Yes, Lord."
Then he touched their eyes. And he said, "It shall be to you as you have believed."
They were now able to see again. Jesus strongly warned them, saying, "Be sure that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it all over the region.
While they were going out, another demon-possessed man was brought to Jesus. The demons were driving him, and he could not speak.
When the devil was cast out, the man spoke. The crowd was astonished. They said, "Such a thing has never been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the prince of demons." There are only a few laborers
Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages. He taught in their synagogues, and he preached the gospel of the kingdom. He healed every disease and affliction.
When he saw the crowds, he felt a profound concern for them. They were defeated and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is great, but the laborers are few.
Ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest field.
Jesus Sends Out the Twelve Disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to him. And he gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal every disease and every affliction.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. The first is Simon Peter, and his brother Andrew. Then comes James, son of Zebedee, and his brother John.
After that were Philip and Bartholomew, also Thomas and Matthew the publican. Two others were James the son of Alphaeus, and Thaddaeus.
The last two are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who later delivered Jesus into the hands of his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve out with this instruction: "Don't go among the Gentiles," he said. "Don't enter any Samaritan town."
Instead, go to the Israelites. They are like lost sheep.
As you go, spread this message: "The Kingdom of Heaven is near."
Healing the sick. Raising the dead. Bringing cleansing to those suffering from skin diseases. Driving out demons. I received freely, so I give freely.
You shall not carry with you gold or silver or copper in your purses.
Do not take a suitcase for the trip. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker should be given what he needs.
When you enter a city or village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay at that person's home until you leave.
When you enter the house, greet those who live in it.
If that house welcomes you, then bless it with peace. Otherwise, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to what you say. If they do not, shake the dust from your feet as you leave that house or that town.
What I am going to tell you now is the truth. On the Day of Judgment, Sodom and Gomorrah will be in better condition than that city.
I am sending you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be as shrewd as snakes and as innocent as doves.
Beware! The men will hand you over to the local courts. They will flog you in their synagogue.
You will be presented to the judges and the kings because of me. You will be witnesses against them and against the non-Jews.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you will say or how you will say it. At that time, the right words will be given to you to say.
It will not be you who speaks, but the spirit of your father will speak through you.
Brothers will deliver their brothers to be killed. Fathers will deliver their children. Children will rise up against their fathers and kill them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But whoever holds on until the end will be saved.
When people attack you in one place, flee to another. What I tell you is true. You will not finish going through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
The student is not better than his teacher. And the servant is not better than his master.
It is enough for the student to be like his teacher, and it is enough for the servant to be like his master. If the head of the household is called Beelzebul, what will the others who live there expect?
Fear not your enemies. What is hidden will be revealed. What is concealed will be made known.
What I tell you in the dark, speak in the light. What was whispered in your ear, shout from the rooftops.
Fear not those who kill the body and cannot kill the soul, but rather fear the one who is able to destroy both body and soul in hellfire.
Is it not sold, two sparrows for only a farthing? Yet not one of them falls to the ground except with the knowledge of your father.
Until he counts every hair on your head!
Do not be afraid. You are more valuable than many sparrows.
What about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about someone who says in front of others that they don't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to make "sons against their fathers. Daughters will refuse to obey their mothers. Nuclei will be against their mothers-in-law.
The man's enemies will be from his own family. (Micah 7:6)
Whoever loves a father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. And whoever loves a son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
And whoever does not take up their cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
If anyone finds his life, he will lose it. If anyone loses his life because of me, he will find it.
He who welcomes you welcomes me, and he who welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me.
If we assume that someone receives a prophet as a prophet, they will receive the reward of a prophet. And if we assume that someone receives a pious person as a pious person, they will receive the reward of a pious person.
If anyone gives even a glass of cold water to a little child who is following me, what I am going to tell you now is true. He will certainly be rewarded.
Jesus and Isa son of Mary.
Jesus completed the education of his twelve disciples. He then moved on to teaching and preaching in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who was to come, or shall we look for someone else?"
Jesus answered, "Go back to John. Tell him what you hear and what you see."
The blind are given sight. The disabled walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are raised. And glad tidings are preached to the poor.
"The blessed are those who do not abandon their faith because of me."
While John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowd about John. He said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? Was it a reed shaken by the wind?"
So what did you come out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. People who dress in fine clothes live in kings' palaces.
What then did you go out into the desert to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one mentioned in the Holy Bible. It says: "I will send my messenger to prepare the way before me." (Malachi 3:1)
What I am going to tell you is the truth. No one has ever been born who is more important than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is more important than he is.
From the days of John the Baptist, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing. And those violent men seize it by force.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
If you are willing to accept that, then John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
Let him who has ears hear.
Who do I compare people to today? They are like children sitting in the city markets, calling out to others. They say:
We played the nay for you. But you did not dance. We sang a funeral song. But you did not become sad.
When John came, he did not eat or drink as you do. And people say, "He has a demon."
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank as you do. And people said, "This man is a glutton and a drunkard, and a friend of tax collectors and 'sinners.' "But wisdom is justified by her deeds." Do not move away from the cities of sin.
Jesus began to speak out against the cities where he had done most of his miracles. The people there had not turned away from their sins. And he said:
How terrifying it would be for you, O Cyrene! How terrifying it would be for you, O Bethsaida! If the achievements realized in you had been realized in Tyre and Sidon, they would have long repented of their sins. They would have put on mourning clothes. They would have sat in ashes.
But I tell you this: the Day of Judgment will be easier for Tyre and Sidon than for you.
And you, Capernaum, what will be your fate? Will you be raised to the heavens? No! But you will be brought down to the place of the dead. Imagine if the miracles that happened in you had happened in Sodom, it would still be standing to this day.
But I tell you this: the day of judgment will be easier for Sodom than for you. "Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest."
Jesus said at that time, "I thank you, Father. You are the Lord of heaven and earth. You have hidden these things from the wise and the scholars, and you have revealed them to the little children."
Yes, Dad. This is what I wanted.
My father gave me everything. The father is the only one who knows the son. And the only ones who know the father are the son and those whom the son chooses to reveal him to.
Come to me, all of you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
They became my slaves and learned from me. I am gentle and free from pride. You will find rest for your soul.
My service is easy and my burden is light.
Jesus is the Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus walked through the grain fields. His disciples were hungry. So they began to pull heads of grain and eat them.
The Pharisees saw this. They said to Jesus, "Look! It is forbidden to do this on the Sabbath. But your disciples are doing it anyway!"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read what David did? He and his men were hungry.
He entered the house of God. He and his men ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it.
Haven't you read the law? It tells us that the priests in the temple are to perform their duties every sabbath day. But they are not considered guilty.
I am telling you that the one who is more important than the temple is here.
The inscription says: "I desire mercy, not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). You do not know what these words mean. If you knew, you would not accuse the innocent.
The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.
From that place, Jesus entered their temple.
There was a man who had a withered and crooked hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they asked him, "Is it lawful for us to heal people on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep falls into a pit on the Sabbath? Wouldn't you seize it and pull it out?"
A man is more valuable than a sheep! So the law allows us to do good on the Sabbath."
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and it was restored, like a new hand, completely like the other hand.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus. The chosen servant of God.
Jesus knew all about the schemes of the Pharisees. So he left that place. Many people followed him, and he healed all their sick.
But he advised them not to tell anyone who he was.
This was to fulfill what had been spoken through the prophet Isaiah, who said:
He is my servant, whom I have chosen, upon whom my soul delights. I have put my Spirit upon him, he will proclaim justice to the nations. He will not quarrel, nor cry out, nor will anyone hear his voice in the streets. The humble of mankind will feast from his abundance, for he is the Lord our God.
He will not argue or shout. His voice will not be heard by anyone in the streets.
He will not break a bent branch. He will not extinguish a dim flame. He will fix everything.
The nations shall put their hope in him." (Isaiah 42:1-4) Jesus and Baseball
A man possessed by demons was brought to Jesus. The man was blind and unable to speak. Jesus healed him. After that, the man was able to speak and see.
All the people were amazed and were saying, "Can this be the Son of David?"
The Pharisees heard that, and they said, "This man casts out demons by the power of Beelzebul, the ruler of the demons."
Jesus knew their thoughts. So he said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation; and every city or house divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom endure?
You say that you cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul. By whose power, then, do *your* people cast out demons? So they will become your judges.
But what if the demons are cast out by the Spirit of God? For the Kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or consider this. How can you enter a powerful man's house and simply take what he owns? You first have to tie him up. Then you can rob his house.
He who is not with me is against me, and he who does not gather the sheep with me scatters them.
Thus I tell you: All sin and every evil word spoken against God will be forgiven. But speaking evil things against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Whoever speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven. A person like that will not be forgiven, either now or in the future.
If you make a good tree, its fruits will be good. And if you make a bad tree, its fruits will be bad. You can know the tree from its fruits.
O den of vipers! How can you, you evil one, speak anything good? For out of the abundance of the heart, the mouth speaks.
A good man says good things, and they come from the goodness preserved within him. And an evil man says evil things, and they come from the evil preserved within him.
But here is what I tell you. On the day of reckoning, people will be asked about every frivolous word they spoke.
"In your words, you will be condemned or acquitted." "The miraculous sign of Yunus"
Some Pharisees and law experts came to Jesus and said, "Teacher, we want to see a miraculous sign from you."
He answered, "Evil and adulterous people seek a sign! But no sign will be given except the sign of Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a great fish three days and three nights. Something like this will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the tomb.
The men of Nineveh will rise up in the judgment with this generation, and will condemn it; for if the mighty works which were done among them had been done in Sodom, it would have remained to this day. Now then, here is one greater than Jonah is here.
The Queen of the South will rise on the day of judgment with the people who are now living, and she will prove them guilty. She came from a very long way to witness the wisdom of Solomon. And now here is someone more important than Solomon.
What happens when an evil jinn emerges from a human? It wanders through dry areas searching for a place to rest. But it does not find it.
Then he says, "I will return to the house I left." And when he reaches it, he finds the house empty. It has been completely cleaned and tidied.
Then the devil goes away and takes with him seven other spirits more wicked than he is. They enter and live there. That man becomes in a worse state than he was before. This is how it will be with the wicked people in our time." The brothers and sisters of Jesus.
While Jesus was still talking to the crowd, his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to talk to him.
Someone told him: "Your mother and your brothers are standing outside. They want to speak to you."
Jesus replied to him, saying, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples and said, "Behold my mother! Behold my brothers!"
Whoever does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother or sister or mother.
The Farmer's Story
On the same day, Jesus left the house and sat on the shore of the Sea of Galilee.
Large crowds gathered around him. So he entered a boat. He sat in it. Everyone stood on the shore.
Then he told them many stories. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds.
Sow the seeds on the ground. Some of them fell on a path. Birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there was not much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil was not deep.
When dawn broke, the sun burned the plants. They dried out because they had no roots.
Other seeds fell among the thorns; these grew and intertwined to overwhelm the plants.
Other seeds fell on good soil. They produced a harvest that yielded 100, 60, and 30 times more than what was sown.
He who has ears, let him hear.
The students came to him and asked him, "Why do you use stories when you address people?"
He answered, saying: "I have been given an opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. It has not been given to those outside."
To everyone who has this type of knowledge, more will be given, and they will indeed receive much. As for those who do not have this type of knowledge, even the little they have will be taken away from them.
This is why I use parables when I speak to people. I say, "They look, but they don't really see. They listen, but they don't really hear or understand."
In it the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled. He said, "You will hear but not understand, and you will see but not perceive what you are seeing."
The hearts of these people have become stubborn. They barely hear with their ears. They have closed their eyes. If not, perhaps they would see with their eyes. Perhaps they would hear with their ears. Perhaps they would understand with their hearts. Perhaps they would return to the Lord, and then he would heal them.' (Isaiah 6:9,10)
But blessed are your eyes, for they see; and blessed are your ears, for they hear.
What I am going to tell you now is real. Many prophets and righteous people wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! Here is the meaning of the farmer's story.
People hear the message about the kingdom but do not understand it. Then the devil comes. He steals what was planted in their hearts. Those people are like seed sown along the path.
Others received the seed that fell on rocky places. These are the ones who hear the message and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no roots, so they last only for a short time. They quickly fall away from the faith when damage or suffering comes because of the message.
Others received the seed that fell among the thorns. These are the ones who hear the message. But then, the worries of this life and the false promises of wealth choke them. So they are not given a chance to produce fruit.
But others received the seed that fell on good soil. They are those who hear the message and understand it. So they produce a crop that yields from 100 to 60 to 30 times more than the farmer planted." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told the assembly another parable: "Thus is the Kingdom of Heaven," he said. "A man sowed good seeds in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat started to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The owner's servants came to him and said, "Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where then have these weeds come from?"
'Enemy did this,' he answered. 'The servants asked him, 'Do you want us to go and remove the weeds?'
"No," the owner replied. "While you are weeding, you might pull up the wheat along with it."
Let it grow together until harvest. At that time I will tell the workers what to do. This is what I will tell them. First gather the weeds. Bind them in bundles to burn. Then gather the wheat. Bring it to my barn." "Stories of the mustard seed and the yeast"
Jesus told the crowds another parable, saying, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took the seed and planted it in a field."
It is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it is the biggest plant in the garden of all. It becomes a tree. The birds come and rest in its branches.
Their Jesus told another story. He said, "The Kingdom of Heaven is like yeast." She said, "A woman mixed it with a large amount of flour. The yeast worked through the whole dough."
Jesus spoke all these things to the crowds in parables. He did not speak to them without a parable.
The words of the Prophet have been fulfilled. He said, "I will open my mouth in parables; I will utter things hidden since the creation of the world." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus explains the parable of the weeds.
Then Jesus went away from the crowds and entered the house. His disciples came to him. They said to him, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man.
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the devil.
The enemy who plants them is the devil. And the harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are pulled up and burned in the fire. Thus will it be in the day of judgment.
The Son of Man will send his angels, who will remove from his kingdom all that causes sin. They will also remove everyone who does evil.
They will throw them into the burning bath. There people will wail and shriek with their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine as a light in the Kingdom of their Father. Let him who has ears hear. Stories of the hidden treasure and the pearl.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he hid it again, went away, and sold all he had and bought that field.
Again, the Kingdom of Heaven is like a merchant in search of rare pearls.
He found one of great value. So he went and left everything he had and bought that pearl. The story of the net.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. It was cast into the lake, and gathered every kind of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen drew the net to shore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But they cast the bad fish away.
Thus will the Day of Judgment be. The angels will come. They will separate those who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
The evil people will be thrown into the burning oven. There the evil ones will bark and scream with their fangs.
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes," they answered.
He told them, "Every scribe who is a student of the law knows the kingdoms of heaven as a homeowner. He brings out new treasures from his storehouse, in addition to the old ones." A prophet without honor.
Jesus finished telling these stories, then moved on from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began to teach people in their synagogue. They were amazed. "Where did this man come from, with this wisdom? Where did he get this power to do miracles?" they asked.
Isn't this the son of the carpenter? Isn't his mother called Mary? Aren't his brothers James, Joseph, Simon and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They were not at all satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor, except in his own country, and not in his own house."
He performed some miracles there simply because they were not believers.
John the Baptist's head was cut off.
At that time, Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
He said to his servant, "This is John the Baptist, he has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to do miracles."
Herod arrested John, bound him and put him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod Philip's brother.
John was saying to Herod, "It is against the law for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they considered John to be a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
He had sworn to her that he would give her what she asked for.
Her mother told her what to say. And the girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very angry. But he remembered his promise and his dinner guests. So he told one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod put John the Baptist to death in prison.
His head was brought on a large platter and given to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowds heard about this. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep concern for them. He healed their sick.
When evening came, the disciples approached him. "This is an isolated place," they said, "and it's already quite late. Send the crowds away so they can go and buy food in the villages."
Jesus answered, "They do not need to leave. Give them something to eat."
"We have only five loaves of bread and two fish," they answered.
"Bring them here to me," he said.
Then Jesus instructed the people to sit on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish. He looked up to heaven and gave thanks. Then He broke the bread and gave it to the disciples. And the disciples gave it to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. The students took 12 baskets of leftovers.
About 5000 men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on water.
Immediately, Jesus ordered the disciples to get into the boat. He told them to go ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then He dispersed the crowds.
After dismissing them, he went up alone to the top of a mountain to worship. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The ship was already far from the shore. The waves were hitting it hard because the winds were blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to His disciples; He walked on the surface of the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and were astonished. "He is a ghost!" they said, and screamed in fear.
Immediately Jesus called out to them, saying, "Be courageous! I am here. Do not be afraid."
"Lord, are you?" Peter asked. If it is you, tell me to come to you on the water.
Jesus said, "Come." And Peter got out of the boat and walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. He began to sink. He cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Immediately Jesus stretched out His hand and caught him. "Your faith is too weak!" He said. "Why did you doubt Me?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
Then those who were in the boat worshiped Jesus. They said, "You are truly the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and reached the region of Gennesaret.
The men who used to live there recognized Jesus. So they sent a message into all the surrounding countryside. And people brought all the sick to Jesus.
They asked him to allow the sick to touch only the edge of his cloak. And everyone who touched him was healed.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. And they asked him,
Why do your pupils not obey what the elders teach? Your pupils do not wash their hands before eating!
Jesus replied, saying, "And why do you not obey the command of God? You prefer to follow your own teachings!"
The Lord said, "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) And He also said, " 'Whoever curses his father or his mother shall be surely put to death.' " (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you allow people to tell their families: "All the help you have received from us is a gift dedicated to God."
They do not need to honor their parents with this gift. You make the word of God useless in order to follow your own teachings.
O ye hypocrites! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said:
These people honor me with what they say. But their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only human laws." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowds to Him. He said, "Listen and understand."
What comes out of your mouth is what makes you unclean."
Then his disciples came to him, saying, "Do you not see that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants that my Father in heaven has not planted. They will be pulled up by the roots.
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Do you still not understand?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Whatever goes into the mouth, goes into the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. These are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts proceed from the heart. For murder, adultery, and sexual immorality generally originate here. Likewise, theft, false witness, and lying generally come from the heart.
These are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A woman from Canaan lived near Tyre and Sidon. She came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! A demon torments my daughter. She suffers greatly."
Jesus did not utter a word. His disciples came to Him. And they requested Him, saying, "Send her away, for she cries out after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman fell to her knees before him. "Oh Lord! Help me!" she said.
He answered, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
Yes, sir, she said. "But even dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table."
Then Jesus answered and said, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be as you desire." And her daughter was healed at that moment. Jesus Feeds the Four Thousand
Jesus left there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up to the top of a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds came to him. They brought to him people who were blind and those who had disabilities in walking. They also brought the crippled, those who could not speak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak began to speak. The crippled were healed. Those who could not walk began to walk. Those who were blind could see. So the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to him and said, "I have deep concern for these people. They have been with me for three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. If I do, they will faint on their way home."
His disciples answered him, "There is nothing here," they said. "Where would we get enough bread to satisfy such a huge crowd?"
How many loaves do you have? Jesus asked. "Seven, and a few small fish," they answered.
Jesus told the crowd to sit on the ground.
Taking the seven loaves and the fish, He thanked and distributed them to the disciples, and the disciples to the people.
They all ate and were filled. Afterward, the disciples took seven baskets full of leftovers.
The number of men who ate was 4000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowds away, he went up into the boat. And he went to the district near Magadan.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
He answered, saying: "In the evening you look at the sky. You say: 'It will be beautiful. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning she says, "Today will be windy. The sky is red and overcast." She knows what she sees in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
A wicked and adulterous generation seeks for a sign; but no sign shall be given to it, except the sign of Jonah. And Jesus left them and departed. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The disciples crossed over to the other side of the lake. They forgot to take bread.
"Beware," Jesus told them. "Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils discussed this among themselves. They said: "He must be saying that because we didn't bring any bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. He said, "Your faith is very weak! Why are you talking to each other about there being no bread?"
Don't you understand yet? Don't you remember the five loaves of bread for the five thousand? Don't you remember how many baskets you filled with the pieces?
Don't you remember the seven loaves for the 4000? Don't you remember how many baskets you filled with the pieces?
How is it that you do not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then the disciples realized that Jesus was not warning them about the yeast used in bread, but was warning them about the teachings of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went to the region of Caesarea Philippi, and there he asked his disciples, "What do people say about the Son of Man?"
They answered: "Some say John the Baptist; others, Elijah; still others, Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon, son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but My Father who is in heaven."
Here is what I say to you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not be strong enough to destroy it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks about his coming death.
From that time on, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what would happen to him. He told them that he must go to Jerusalem. There, he would suffer greatly at the hands of the elders, the priests, and the legal experts. He would be killed and rise from the dead on the third day.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him, saying, "God shall never allow you to be put to the test!"
Jesus thundered and said to Peter, "Begone from my path, Satan! You savor my path. You have no thought in matters of God, but in matters of men."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples, saying, "Whoever wants to become my follower must deny themselves, take up their cross, and follow me.
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he sacrifices his life for me, he will find a better life.
What good would it do someone to gain the whole world and at the same time lose their soul? Or what could someone possibly exchange their soul for?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and He will reward each person according to his deeds.
What I am going to tell you is the truth. Some of those who are standing here will not die before they see the coming of the Son of Man in His kingdom.
Jesus' appearance changed.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John the brother of James, and led them up onto a high mountain. There they were all alone.
There before them his appearance was transformed. His face shone like the sun. His clothes became white as light of day.
At that very moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make three tabernacles; one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
While Peter was still talking, a bright cloud enveloped them. A voice from the cloud said, "This is my son, whom I love. I am very pleased with him. Listen to him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were terrified and fell face down on the ground.
But Jesus came and touched them. "Rise," he said, "and do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. During the descent, Jesus told them what to do. He said, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen. Wait until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The disciples asked, "Why do the legal experts say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is true. It is necessary for Elijah to come and restore all things again.
But I tell you, Elijah has come already. The people did not recognize him. They did to him everything they wanted to do. In the same way, they will make the Son of Man suffer."
The disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus healed a boy who had a demon.
As they came near the crowd, a man ran up to Jesus; he knelt down before him.
"O Lord," he said, "have mercy on my son. He convulses violently and suffers greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought him to your disciples, but they were not able to heal him.
"O wicked infidels!" Jesus answered. "How much longer must I be with you? How much longer must you put up with me? Bring the boy to me."
Jesus commanded the demon to leave the boy, and it departed from him. And he was healed in that very moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and asked, "Why could we not drive out that demon?"
He answered and said: "Because your faith is too small. What I am about to tell you is the truth. If you have faith as small as a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain: 'Be removed from here to there.' And it will be removed. Nothing will be impossible for you."
